{"author":{"id":"7674679dac9170669f752d932f8b5955b1d55717359e13b1c1e687003c7bb99f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791399223,"nonce":"K/Y+xOAABJGF7FPWacOL7t1vVAI=","title":"Nested list: a child layer is its own table, indented as a unit with its own header; tree and column widths measured over every row","message":"Design in doc/design/terminal-renderer.md, Nesting, \"A nested layer is its own table\" (2026-10-07). The rows under an opened parent are a table of their own: indented as one unit, two cells a level, with its own header above its first row, its own columns in its own widths, id first and flush within the table. Widths are measured over every row of a layer, drawn or hidden, so every table of a layer matches and folding moves nothing. The top header describes the roots and never changes; a child table whose header scrolled off keeps it on the first body line like a group header. The tree cell holds the arrow and count only, as wide as the largest count on any row.\n\nTwo bugs this replaces, both from measuring the drawn rows instead of every row: treeWidth (tui/nest.go) is handed p.order, the visible rows, so unfolding deepens the tree and shifts every column right; widthsOf (tui/list_view.go) does the same, so a repeated layer's parent columns move when its children appear.\n\nThe gantt keeps its indent in the label column and one header; only the width fix applies to it.","files":null,"fields":{"area":["tui"],"priority":"high","status":"to-do","type":"task"}}]}